# Names only, never values (protocol Part 15: «Un `.env.example` con los nombres de las variables
# y sin un solo valor, para que se sepa qué hace falta sin filtrar nada»).
#
# The values live in `.env.local`, which is in `.gitignore` and never in a commit, never in a
# comment and never «temporalmente». Different keys for development and production — which is why
# ADR 0034 §14 creates two Supabase projects rather than pointing both environments at one.
#
# If one of these ever leaks: rotate first, investigate after. Rewriting git history does not
# help; assume it is already out.

# Database (ADR 0012, ADR 0034 — sprint 15)
#
# Supabase's TRANSACTION POOLER, port 6543 — not the direct connection, which is the one the
# dashboard shows first. ADR 0012: a service that hibernates and wakes exhausts direct
# connections. `packages/db/src/connect.ts` refuses a hosted URL on any other port.
DATABASE_URL=

# Supabase (ADR 0034 §3, §7, §14 — sprint 15)
#
# The project URL and the anon key are public by design: they reach the browser, and row-level
# security is what makes that safe. The anon key can only act as `authenticated`.
#
# These two carry the `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix, where this file used to name them without it. The
# prefix is what makes a variable readable from a client component in Next.js, and these are the
# two that have to be. The rename is deliberate and the omission of the prefix on the next one is
# just as deliberate.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The service key bypasses row-level security. Protocol Part 15: «La clave de servicio de la base
# de datos no se usa nunca desde el navegador.» Server only — no `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix, ever, and
# it is what writes the audit log, which no browser may write.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Google sign-in (ADR 0034 §4 — sprint 15)
#
# Configured in the Supabase dashboard rather than read by our code, and listed here so the
# deployment checklist is complete. The app stays in TESTING mode: leaving it needs a homepage on
# a verified domain we own, and `*.onrender.com` is not ours. 100 test users, added by email.
# `access_type: offline` is never requested, so Google's 7-day refresh-token expiry cannot sign
# anyone out — Supabase issues its own.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Stripe, single payment (phase 2)
#
# Still unused, and ADR 0021 is why: charging waits until the product is judged professionally
# sellable, and ADR 0034 released accounts without touching that.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=

# Published site storage (phase 0, publish task)
#
# Unused, and ADR 0008 is why: hosted publishing has no plan and no date, so there is no bucket
# of ours that a client's site lives in. Left standing rather than removed, because retiring
# these names is a decision about that ADR and not about this sprint.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=

# Transactional email (phase 2)
#
# Still unused in sprint 15: password reset rides Supabase's own mail on the free plan, with its
# send limit, and Resend waits for a reason to exist (ADR 0034).
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Errors (phase 1)
SENTRY_DSN=

# Tests only — never set in production
#
# Where `packages/db/test/bootstrap.ts` creates and drops `retorika_db_test`. Defaults to the
# local Postgres on the Unix socket, so on this machine it needs no value at all. There is no
# Docker here and there is not meant to be (protocol Part 18).
RETORIKA_DB_MAINTENANCE_URL=
